"""Echo Isles: two story speeches, and places named where the old accept line used to carry them."""

STORY = {
    'echo_isles_camp_supplies': ("You're the one off the last boat? Good, because I've got a job and nobody to give it to.\n\nI'm Rask'jin, the quartermaster, which out here means I'm the one who has to turn a landing spot into a camp before the season turns. A camp wants a palisade, and a palisade wants posts, and sand won't hold a post, mon. I've driven forty of them and watched every one lean over by morning.\n\nReef coral holds, the deep-red kind that grows where the current's strongest. I can't dive for it, but the Coconut Crabs drag it up the beaches to line their burrows, so the quick way to get coral is to take it back off a crab. They're on the Tidal Flats, far south of the Landing, they're the size of a kodo's head, and they have opinions.", 'Bring the deep-red pieces, not the pale. The pale coral crumbles under a mallet.'),
    'echo_isles_relic_recovery': ("Before da last push on Zalazane, I want somethin' brought back instead of broken. Dey're hexed Darkspear totems, our own work, twisted by his magic, but not so far you can't tell what dey were.\n\nDa voodoo trolls carry dem, and da hex trolls, and da carvers at da camp fires, right here round da village.\n\nBring dem to me. I mend people, and da Elder says what's on dese totems is what's on da ones Zalazane took. I want to see how it was done, because if I understand it, somebody might undo it one day. Don't handle da hex on dem if you can help it, mon.", "Wrap dem in somethin' before you carry dem. Dat hex don't care whose hands it's in."),
    'echo_tarna_kragg': ("Now for the part I haven't said out loud to anyone at the Landing. I lost a skiff last month, and I've let people think it was the tide, because the truth makes me sound like I've been in the sun too long.\n\nIt wasn't the tide. Something the size of a hut lives under the cays on the Tidal Flats. It's a crab, grown fat over forty years on everything the flats leave behind, and the old fishers called him Shellcrusher Kragg. He takes the whole boat when he's hungry and lets the fisherman swim.\n\nHe comes up when the Sand Burrowers trailing him go quiet, so kill them and he'll come looking. Don't try to out-wait him, because he's more patient than you are. Hit him from the side. I want a trophy, and I want my skiff's anchor back.", None),
    'echo_vashj_scales': ("I can read the old runes, but I can't read them off the air. The naga wear them on tide-etched scales, each one marked with a spiral that's half writing and half weather. The spiral changes with the water and the writing doesn't, and I've spent years failing to explain that to anyone.\n\nThe Naga Coastguards at the Naga Ruins carry them, and so do the tidecallers. Take them off the ones who've no further use for them, and bring me enough that I can lay them side by side. One scale is a mark, and a row of them is a sentence.\n\nAnd please wash your hands afterwards. Whatever they cure the scales in won't come off a palm by itself.", None),
    'echo_vashj_warden': ("Warden Ssaleth keeps those ruins the way a lock keeps a door. He does it out of an oath, not out of hatred. That changes nothing about what has to happen, but I'd still rather you knew.\n\nI can feel the oath on him from here, and I can feel that it's fraying. An oath that frays doesn't come apart tidily. It goes all at once, on a night nobody chose. If he falls, that door stays shut a while longer. If he's left alone, the oath gives out by itself, and whatever walks up to the door next will have worse manners and no oath at all.\n\nThin the Naga Coastguards and find him at the Naga Ruins, where the stones are highest. His trident cuts, and when you hold still, he pulls the strength out of you. Don't hold still, and don't fight him standing in the water.", None),
    'echo_dritt_seer': ("Spearfishers I can put a price on. The thing that sings them into position is a different conversation.\n\nShe's called Seer Sshara. She sits on a rock halfway down the Drowned Causeway, and she doesn't let a sailor past, and she's been doing it longer than I've been on this island. I had a crew of seven, good ones, mostly. She charmed every one of them into walking off the end of a pier, in order, while I stood forty feet away shouting, and the last one waved at me.\n\nI'd like to be paid back in gold, and I'll settle for silence. Here's the only advice I've ever given anyone for free: don't listen to her sing. Plug your ears with wax or cloth or anything, and go deaf for the afternoon.", None),
    'echo_kesh_pearls': ("I'm Kesh, and I keep the spirit shrine, these stones a few steps from the temple door. Something's started leaving things on the shrine step that nobody put there.\n\nI sweep that step at dawn. I know what I put on it and I know what the tide puts on it, and for a month now there's been a third answer. The spirits are asking again, and they haven't asked in my lifetime. The old offering was always a tide-touched pearl, grey and damp and impossible to dry.\n\nThe only pearls left are inside the temple. The murlocs hoard them in the shallows, and the tidal elementals wear them like bad jewellery. Go south-west to the Sunken Temple Grotto and in at the temple door, at the end of the Grotto trail, and keep them wet.", None),
}
